NAME
       timedatectl - show and set the clock, time zone and network time sync

SYNOPSIS
       timedatectl [status]
       timedatectl show
       timedatectl list-timezones
       timedatectl set-timezone ZONE
       timedatectl set-ntp true|false
       timedatectl set-time 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'

DESCRIPTION
       timedatectl shows the system clock in local time and UTC, the time
       zone, whether the clock is synchronised over the network (NTP), and
       whether the NTP service is active. It also changes these settings.

       The time zone decides how the clock is shown (local time); the clock
       itself counts in UTC. Zones are named Area/City, such as
       America/New_York or Europe/Paris. Setting the zone points
       /etc/localtime at the zone file under /usr/share/zoneinfo.

       On RHEL 9 network time comes from chronyd. set-ntp true enables and
       starts chronyd; set-ntp false stops and disables it. Which server it
       uses is set in /etc/chrony.conf, not by timedatectl.

COMMANDS
       status Show the current settings (the default).

       show   The same in NAME=VALUE form, for scripts.

       list-timezones
              List every time zone name. Pipe it into grep to find one.

       set-timezone ZONE
              Change the time zone.

       set-ntp BOOL
              Turn network time synchronisation on or off.

       set-time TIME
              Set the clock by hand. This is refused while NTP is on.

EXAMPLES
       Show the clock and whether it is synchronised:

           timedatectl

       Find and set the time zone for Lisbon:

           timedatectl list-timezones | grep -i lisbon
           timedatectl set-timezone Europe/Lisbon

       Turn on network time after configuring /etc/chrony.conf:

           timedatectl set-ntp true
           chronyc sources

NOTES
       "System clock synchronized: yes" appears only after chronyd has
       reached a server; right after a change it may say no for a while.

FILES
       /etc/localtime
              A link to the current time zone file.

SEE ALSO
       chronyc(1), chrony.conf(5), date(1), hwclock(8)
